"MERGE (e:Article {id: \"Article_5084547\", title: \"Digital Homework Support Program for Children and Adolescents With Attention-Deficit/Hyperactivity Disorder: Protocol for a Randomized Controlled Trial\", abstract: \"Background Attention-deficit / hyperactivity disorder ( ADHD ) affects 4 % to 5 % of the general population . Homework sessions are frequent conflictual moments characterized by increased anxiety in children and stress in children parents , contributing to a lower family quality of life . Children with Background Attention-deficit / hyperactivity disorder ( ADHD ) experience more severe homework problems than typically developing peers . Poor academic performance in individuals with Background Attention-deficit / hyperactivity disorder ( ADHD ) is partly attributed to challenging homework . Psychoeducational and school-based approaches are time-consuming and not fully accessible to professionals . Digital tools , such as smartphone and tablet apps , might offer an interesting alternative . We present We digital homework support program for children and adolescents , known as \u201c Programme d\u2019Aide Num\u00e9rique aux Devoirs pour Enfant avec TDA-H \u201d ( PANDAH ) , along with the study protocol of We ongoing randomized controlled trial . Objective our ongoing randomized controlled trial aims to test our digital homework support program for children and adolescents , known as \u201c Programme d\u2019Aide Num\u00e9rique aux Devoirs pour Enfant avec TDA-H \u201d ( PANDAH ) efficacy in improving homework performance and family quality of life . Methods Individuals aged 9-16 years with an Background Attention-deficit / hyperactivity disorder ( ADHD ) diagnosis and no comorbid psychiatric disorders are included . our ongoing randomized controlled trial is a multicenter study involving 9 reference centers for Background Attention-deficit / hyperactivity disorder ( ADHD ) in France . our ongoing randomized controlled trial comprises ( 1 ) a 3-month period with a randomized controlled trial design , where participants are divided into 2 parallel groups ( group 1 : care as usual or waiting list ; group 2 : our digital homework support program for children and adolescents , known as \u201c Programme d\u2019Aide Num\u00e9rique aux Devoirs pour Enfant avec TDA-H \u201d ( PANDAH ) app ) , followed by ( 2 ) an extension period of 3 months ( months 3-6 ) , during which all participants will have access to our digital homework support program for children and adolescents , known as \u201c Programme d\u2019Aide Num\u00e9rique aux Devoirs pour Enfant avec TDA-H \u201d ( PANDAH ) . an extension period of 3 months ( months 3-6 ) serves as a crucial incentive for patients initially randomly assigned to group 1 : care as usual or waiting list . Assessments will be conducted at baseline , month 3 , and month 6 for each patient by trained psychologists . The primary end point will be the global Homework Performance Questionnaire ( HPQ ) , Parent version score at 6 months . The main analysis will adhere to the \u201c intent-to-treat principle \u201d ( all patient data will be analyzed according to all patient data initial group determined by randomization ) . We expect ( 1 ) HPQ score improvement in individuals using our digital homework support program for children and adolescents , known as \u201c Programme d\u2019Aide Num\u00e9rique aux Devoirs pour Enfant avec TDA-H \u201d ( PANDAH ) during the first 3-month period compared to individuals not using our digital homework support program for children and adolescents , known as \u201c Programme d\u2019Aide Num\u00e9rique aux Devoirs pour Enfant avec TDA-H \u201d ( PANDAH ) ; ( 2 ) greater HPQ score improvement for individuals using our digital homework support program for children and adolescents , known as \u201c Programme d\u2019Aide Num\u00e9rique aux Devoirs pour Enfant avec TDA-H \u201d ( PANDAH ) for 6 months compared to those using our digital homework support program for children and adolescents , known as \u201c Programme d\u2019Aide Num\u00e9rique aux Devoirs pour Enfant avec TDA-H \u201d ( PANDAH ) for 3 months only ; and ( 3 ) adherence to our digital homework support program for children and adolescents , known as \u201c Programme d\u2019Aide Num\u00e9rique aux Devoirs pour Enfant avec TDA-H \u201d ( PANDAH ) , measured with in-app metrics . Results Recruitment began in January 2024 , and our ongoing randomized controlled trial is ongoing . Conclusions our ongoing randomized controlled trial contributes to the digital transformation of health care . The use of smartphone apps in self-care and self-management is a societal phenomenon , and The use of smartphone apps in self-care and self-management implementation in the field of psychiatry is of particular interest . The app might serve as both valuable support for patients and an opportunity for parents to distance parents from conflict-laden homework sessions . Since the market for smartphone apps in the health care and well-being sector is primarily industry driven , it is crucial to have an academic conception and evaluation of such digital tools . Trial Registration ClinicalTrials.gov NCT04857788 ; [ URL ] International Registered Report Identifier ( IRRID ) PRR1-10.2196/44553\", labels: [], teams: [\"LS2N-DUKE\"]});"
