"MERGE (e:Article {id: \"Article_5244508\", title: \"A new incremental pipeline for concept formation driven by prior knowledge: Application on the AI Act domain\", abstract: \"In the Ontology Learning research domain , despite recent advancements , the performance of current non or semi-supervised approaches for concept formation remains sub-optimal , particularly from a single , small-sized corpus for a specialized domain . In order to answer the performance drawback , this paper introduces a novel pipeline , called CO-ISSC ( Core Ontology-based Incremental Semi-Supervised Clustering ) , for concept formation towards ontology learning . a novel pipeline , called CO-ISSC ( Core Ontology-based Incremental Semi-Supervised Clustering ) uses a PLM ( Pre-trained Language Model ) and combines in an incremental manner a semi-supervised dimension reduction technique and a clustering technique , guided by core concepts as prior knowledge to align results with the ontology domain . a novel pipeline , called CO-ISSC ( Core Ontology-based Incremental Semi-Supervised Clustering ) incremental nature enhances prior knowledge and boosts a novel pipeline , called CO-ISSC ( Core Ontology-based Incremental Semi-Supervised Clustering ) performance . a novel pipeline , called CO-ISSC ( Core Ontology-based Incremental Semi-Supervised Clustering ) performance is evaluated on the recent and significant AI Act text established by the European Union , which aims to ensure the safety , transparency , and non-discrimination of AI systems . To this end , we manually built a benchmark terminology for the ontology domain given that no reference model exists yet . The results demonstrate promising performance of a novel pipeline , called CO-ISSC ( Core Ontology-based Incremental Semi-Supervised Clustering ) , outperforming baseline non-supervised or semi-supervised approaches such as DBSCAN , similarity measure based approaches , SVM and ANN .\", labels: [\"Industrial Digitalization\"], teams: [\