"MERGE (e:Article {id: \"Article_4592032\", title: \"Sampling based sequential dependencies discovery in Higher-Order Network Models\", abstract: \"Higher-order networks are a general form of network models that include memory nodes used to capture indirect dependencies between entities . When built from sequential or pathway data , random walks performed on Higher-order networks usually represent input sequences better than traditional first-order models . Unlike traditional first-order models , there are various ways to build higher-order networks that already exist in the literature . We introduce a new variable-order network where nodes can encode sequences of varying length . Nodes are selected by looking at sub-sequences that are unlikely extensions of already detected sub-sequences . Using experiments on real-world datasets , We demonstrate that a new variable-order network where nodes can encode sequences of varying length produces smaller and as accurate models compared to the main variable-order model in the literature . We also study the differences achieved when ranking items using a higher-order reformulation of the PageRank metric .\", labels: [], teams: [\"LS2N-DUKE\"]});"
