"MERGE (e:Article {id: \"Article_4081227\", title: \"EMBEDD-ER : EMBEDDing Educational Resources Using Linked Open Data\", abstract: \"There are a lot of educational resources publicly available online . Recommender systems and information retrieval engines can help learners and educators navigate in a lot of educational resources publicly available online . However , a lot of educational resources publicly available online differ in format , size , type , topics , etc . differ complicate a lot of educational resources publicly available online use and manipulation which raised the need for having a common representation for educational resources and texts in general . Efforts have been made by the research community to create various techniques to homogeneously represent a lot of educational resources publicly available online . Although various techniques to homogeneously represent these resources have achieved incredible results in many tasks , various techniques to homogeneously represent these resources seem to be dependent on the writing style and not only on the content . Furthermore , various techniques to homogeneously represent these resources do not generate representations that reflect a semantic representation of the content . In this work , we present a new task-agnostic method ( EMBEDD-ER ) to generate representations for educational resources based on document annotation and Linked Open Data ( LOD ) . a new task-agnostic method ( EMBEDD-ER ) to generate representations for educational resources based on document annotation and Linked Open Data ( LOD ) creates representations that are focused on the content , compact , and can be generalized to unseen resources without requiring extra training . The resulting representations encapsulate the information found in a lot of educational resources publicly available online and project similar resources closer to one another than to non-similar ones . Empirical tests have shown promising results both visually and in a subject classification task .\", labels: [], teams: [\"LS2N-DUKE\"]});"
