"MERGE (e:Article {id: \"Article_4139744\", title: \"A methodology to compare XAI explanations on natural language processing\", abstract: \"Recent advances in eXplainable Artificial Intelligence ( XAI ) led to many different methods to improve the explainability of deep learning algorithms . There are nowadays many options at hand , and adapting existing ones to new problems may be needed . Hence , one may find oneself in a struggle to choose the right method to generate explanations . Considered explanations may be a set of important features or factual and counterfactual explanations . This chapter presents two protocols to compare different XAI methods . The first is designed to be applied when no end-users are available . An objective , quantitative metric is being compared to an objective data expert 's analysis . The second experiment is designed to take into account end-users feedback . The second experiment uses the quantitative metric applied in The first and compares the quantitative metric applied in the first experiment to users ' preferences . Then , the quantitative metric applied in the first experiment can be used to evaluate explanations , allowing to try multiple explanation methods and adjust multiple explanation methods , without the cost of having a systematic end-users evaluation . The second experiment can be applied to post-hoc explaining approaches as well as to self-explaining neural networks . Knowing if an explanation makes sense to end-users helps assess the explanation system but also the prediction the prediction itself . This chapter is focused on the methodology to assess such explanations . the methodology to assess such explanations helps fasten and reduce the cost of manual result analysis during system design . The first shows that IOU can be used to focus on instances where compared explanations give different results . The second experiment shows IOU is not representative of end-users ' preferences , and gives clues on how to improve the definition of IOU .\", labels: [\"Industrial Digitalization\", \"User-Centered Design\"], teams: [\"LS2N-DUKE\"]});"
