"MERGE (e:Article {id: \"Article_3197664\", title: \"SINr: Fast Computing of Sparse Interpretable Node Representations is not a Sin!\", abstract: \"While graph embedding aims at learning low-dimensional representations of nodes encompassing the graph topology , word embedding focus on learning word vectors that encode semantic properties of the vocabulary . graph embedding finds applications on tasks such as link prediction and node classification while word embedding is systematically considered in natural language processing . Most of the time , graph and word embeddings are considered on graph and word embeddings own as distinct tasks . However , word co-occurrence matrices , widely used to extract word embeddings , can be seen as graphs . Furthermore , most network embedding techniques rely either on a word embedding methodology ( Word2vec ) or on matrix factorization , also widely used for word embedding . most network embedding techniques are usually computationally expensive , parameter dependant and the dimensions of the embedding space are not interpretable . To circumvent these issues , we introduce the Lower Dimension Bipartite Graphs Framework ( LDBGF ) which takes advantage of the fact that all graphs can be described as bipartite graphs , even in the case of textual data . This underlying bipartite structure may be explicit , like in coauthor networks . However , with LDBGF , we focus on uncovering latent bipartite structures , lying for instance in social or word co-occurrence networks , and especially such structures providing conciser and interpretable representations of the graph at hand . We further propose SINr , an efficient implementation of the LDBGF approach that extracts Sparse Interpretable Node Representations using community structure to approximate This underlying bipartite structure . In the case of graph embedding , We near-linear time method is the fastest of We benchmark , parameter-free and provides state-of-the-art results on the classical link prediction task . We also show that low-dimensional vectors can be derived from We using singular value decomposition . In the case of word embedding , our near-linear time method proves to be very efficient considering the classical similarity evaluation .\", labels: [], teams: [\"LS2N-DUKE\"]});"
