"MERGE (e:Article {id: \"Article_2891862\", title: \"Machine Learning and Statistical Verification for Security\", abstract: \"The main objective of this thesis is to combine the advantages of probabilistic graphical model learning and formal verifica-tion in order to build a novel strategy for secu-rity assessments . The second objective is to assess the security of a given system by veri-fying whether a given system satisfies given properties and , if not , how far is a given system from satisfying given properties . We are interested in performing formal verification of a given system based on event sequences col-lected from a given system execution . Consequently , We propose a model-based approach where a Re-cursive Timescale Graphical Event Model ( RT-GEM ) , learned from the event streams , is con-sidered to be representative of the underlying system . a Re-cursive Timescale Graphical Event Model ( RT-GEM ) , learned from the event streams is then used to check a se-curity property . If a se-curity property is not verified , We propose a search methodology to find an-other close model that satisfies a se-curity property . We discuss and justify the different techniques we use in We approach and we adapt a distance mea-sure between Graphical Event Models . a distance mea-sure between Graphical Event Models gives an insight on how far our real system is from verifying a se-curity property . For the sake of completeness , We propose series of exper-iments on synthetic data allowing to provide experimental evidence that we can attain the desired goals .\", labels: [\"Monitoring and Analysis\", \"Industrial Digitalization\"], teams: [\"LS2N-DUKE\"]});"
